<section class="page-hero compact"><div class="wrap"><nav class="crumbs"><a href="../index.html">Home</a> / <a href="index.html">Tools</a> / <span>Coordinates</span></nav><h1>Coordinate converter</h1><p class="lede">Paste coordinates in any common format, click the map, or use your current location.</p></div></section>
<section class="wrap" id="tool" data-tool="coords">
  <form id="coord-form" class="guess-form"><label class="sr" for="coord-in">Coordinates</label><input id="coord-in" placeholder='48.8584, 2.2945  or  48°51′30″N 2°17′40″E'><button class="btn btn-accent" type="submit">Convert</button></form>
  <button class="btn btn-sm btn-ghost" id="me" type="button">Use my location</button> <a class="btn btn-sm btn-ghost" id="osm" href="#" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open in OpenStreetMap</a>
  <div class="tool-out" aria-live="polite"></div>
  <div class="tool-map" id="tool-map"></div>
</section>
<section class="wrap prose"><h2>Coordinate formats explained</h2><p><b>Decimal degrees</b> (48.8584, 2.2945) are what most apps and APIs use: positive latitude is north, positive longitude is east. <b>Degrees, minutes, seconds</b> (48° 51′ 30″ N) split each degree into 60 minutes and each minute into 60 seconds — still standard on nautical charts and in aviation. <b>UTM</b> divides the world into 60 north–south zones, each 6° wide, and gives positions in metres east and north, which makes measuring distances on paper maps easy. A <b>geohash</b> encodes a location as a short string; each extra character narrows the box, so nine characters pin a spot to a few metres. Read our full <a href="../guides/latitude-longitude-explained.html">guide to latitude and longitude</a>.</p></section>
